now to our exclusive reporting on new  york governor andrew cuomo and his  deadly nursing home scandal we are  hearing  a new side to his decision to force  nursing homes to accept patients  infected with coronavirus for the first  time a nursing home administrator is  speaking on camera  about the governor's controversial order  that covid patients be put in long-term  care facilities  rather than in hospitals aisha hosni  spoke with that administrator  my position from the get-go was  we can't do it michael krauss is the  administrator of silver lake specialized  care center in staten island new york  and for the first time  he and his team are telling us what  really happened behind closed doors when  governor andrew cuomo  issued that controversial march 25th  order  it required all nursing homes to take in  covet patients  released from overwhelmed hospitals he  says he first heard about it on a covet  conference call  with other administrators hospital  leaders and state officials  i said that's ridiculous everyone heard  about the story that happened in  washington state  and you know that was enough for me to  to understand  i can't put these people at risk we  can't be doing this  it's it's just not right to the  residents and you vocalize that  on these phone conversations i did  vocalize it  and then you know once it was shot down  i never spoke again there are more  important people than me  who thought it was a good idea who was  it shot down by  i couldn't tell you some of the it was  on a conference call  i don't know kraus says many other  administrators  rang the alarm and then just four days  into the new order  the nation's largest association  representing long-term and post-acute  care providers  pushed out this stark warning we  strongly object to this policy directive  it reads  this action by a state will put the many  frail and older adults who reside in  nursing homes at risk  the group even offered recommendations  to reduce that risk  we think that the governors in every  state  follow that advice with the exception of  new york  at the time krauss feared his facility  didn't have enough ppe  he says his vendors told him they had to  prioritize hospitals  so he followed his gut and locked down  we would take back our own patients  because that's only fair  but to take on new cases i felt was  unfair  so we shut down the entire operation we  had no admissions for about 10 weeks  maybe longer i don't remember and  i mean that was my way of following the  governor's order  but refusing to take in patients from  hospitals  came with consequences many facilities  were  were threatened by hospitals to take the  covert patients  threatened in what way well i mean again  i never saw the exact  emails but they were threatened that  they um they would be reported  for non-compliance um  i think in in our case we weren't  allowed  we were told please don't send us your  sick patients  there were multiple hospitals that were  threatening they were saying don't  bother sending us your patients  wow wow in the end  silver lake reported two confirmed  coveted deaths at the facility  six out of facility and 37 presumed  covet deaths  the numbers are not the lowest in the  state but far  from the highest so you following your  gut do you think that made a difference  knowing my population i think i would i  we would have we would have had a lot  more loss of life  there has to be a better regard for the  elderly population and the  population of residents in nursing homes  that are sick they're vulnerable and i  think  they should have been given a much  higher priority  in may of 2020 governor cuomo amended  his nursing home order  now hospitalized residents must test  negative before being readmitted to a  nursing home  but was the damage already done did the  march  25 directive put these facilities in an  unreasonable position  they were petrified but they were more  petrified of the department of health  new york's attorney general found  insufficient ppe  and testing at nursing homes put  residents at  increased risk of harm fifteen thousand  people  died in nursing homes and long-term care  facilities  governor cuomo repeatedly insists his  order  was not the driving cause do you think  that's fair do you believe that  so not entirely i really feel  that when the hospitals kept on pushing  us to take these residents i think that  was a very big mistake  our many thanks to michael krause for  speaking on camera  about this aisha hosni did that  interview she joins us live now aisha  thank you  we learned a lot there what did cuomo  know about the warnings  sandra that's a great question obviously  you heard  michael krause in that piece basically  say look there were state officials in  these  uh coveted conference calls when i spoke  up so we have to assume that there were  state officials that heard  his warnings his uh ringing the alarm  we did reach out to the governor's  office to find out if he was aware of  this we haven't heard back yet  the department of health though did  respond to our request for common here's  what they say they say  that march 25th guidance they're calling  it  was not a directive to accept covid19  patients appoint the attorney general's  office  finds the nursing homes understood so  that's what they're saying and  essentially  what they're saying is that the ag found  that it was  reasonable for nursing homes to have  understood don't take these patients if  you're not adequately prepared now that  last part adequately prepared  that was part of the federal guidance  coming down from the cdc  it was not included in the state's order  that march 25th order so obviously some  people out there would say  nursing homes uh perhaps did not take it  that way  yeah and yet cuomo insists that he  followed cdc guidance in disorder so  the big picture question here aisha is  did cuomo's order lead to an increase  in nursing home deaths from covid where  do we stand on that question  yeah i'm glad you asked that because i  feel like that question has really  gotten  lost as we've sort of gone on this path  that's  you know winding in all sorts of  directions as all these controversies  kind of arise around the governor  the answer is we don't know and we don't  know that anyone is really looking into  that  really important and serious question we  know that the department of justice  began to look into it  back in august they were actually just  looking into whether they should launch  an investigation into that question or  not  we're not aware that they ever did we  know that yesterday though  several lawmakers here in new york  including congresswoman  elise stefanik she did call out for the  doj  to go ahead and now launch an  investigation  into that question she wants the doj to  find out if  this order violates the civil rights of  those residents that were living  in these group homes great reporting  aisha  yeah thank you for bringing that to us  you bet
 

